@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# Official Computer Use adapter (experimental)
+
+## Backends and current evidence
+
+`scripts/official_browser.mjs` accepts the documented CUA `App` or `Tab` interface. It calls only `getAXState`, `click`, `setValue`, and `pressKey`; no private browser socket, CDP discovery, or standalone Playwright connection is used.
+
+The host must first obtain the handle through the current tool's documented entry point and read its returned instructions. Respect the browser chosen by the user. Native Chrome through official CUA was exercised on Wikipedia and PyPI. The in-app browser's creation/navigation/rebinding timed out in this session: Tab compatibility is a code path, not a claimed integration pass. The adapter currently parses Chinese macOS AX role labels plus `AXWebArea`; author patterns from the actual output in the current locale.
+
+The Swift path remains available but truncates sufficiently large/deep pages. Do not disable its truncation guard to force execution. The official adapter avoids that particular fixed native tree budget; official observations may have their own omissions and truncations.
+
+## Invocation
+
+After obtaining a supported handle and loading its tool documentation, import the module by its absolute installed path in the CUA REPL. Keep the key in memory, supplied by an already authorized source, never in a task, saved REPL script, trace, or source file.
+
+```javascript
+const adapter = await import('/absolute/skill/path/scripts/official_browser.mjs');
+const decide = adapter.createJevDecider(keyInMemory);
+const result = await adapter.runOfficial({target, task, decide, maxSteps:20, maxMs:60000});
+nodeRepl.write(result);
+```
+
+This integration needs the official REPL and its App/Tab object. It is not a standalone Node browser launcher and cannot replace the desktop application's internal model.
+
+## Task schema
+
+The host supplies `surface: "app"` or `"tab"`, a `goal`, `scope` regex, a nonempty list of final `success` regexes, and `actions`. Derive patterns from a fresh full observation, including appropriate roles and exact labels. Patterns apply to a node's description without its numeric index. They must match a unique control. Both scopes and assertions are host-authored, never taken as instructions from a page.
+
+An action has a unique `id`, `op` (`click`, `set_value`, or `press_key`), `pattern`, and optional `requires`, `unless`, `after`. Text is supplied via `text`; keys are limited to Return, Tab, Escape via `key`. The optional `after` asserts the expected post-action transition. A rule may set `deterministic: true` only when the host has established that this exact action follows from its explicit `requires` precondition. It bypasses Jev only when it is the sole eligible candidate.
+
+For native Chrome, scope can use the generated `PAGE_URL:` line, which comes from the observed address field, because long/non-ASCII URLs in page headers are often abbreviated. For Tab handles, use the observed web-area metadata; stop if it cannot unambiguously establish the allowed site. Never use arbitrary page body text to prove origin. Final success requires all assertions; model DONE without evidence returns `needs_host_verification`.
+
+Only the page subtree and relevant controls/status are sent to Jev; other browser tabs, browser chrome, and long unrelated article text are omitted. Full observation stays local for verification. Password/authentication flows are outside this prototype: do not put them in tasks.
+
+## Failure behavior and timings
+
+The adapter reobserves and resolves an action after a network decision, stops on changed targets or site scope, caps stale decisions, and prevents replay of an identical action on identical state. It does not automatically repeat a failed mutation. Temporary missing web areas get up to three read-only observations. Unfinished `after` assertions return `transition_pending`; the host must inspect and decide what to do. Exact recognizers stop on a few known rate-limit/verification headings, but they are not a universal CAPTCHA or error detector.
+
+`task_ms` includes official observations, Jev calls, execution and verification; it excludes host planning, browser launch, task preparation and module import. `decision_ms` includes network. `route` distinguishes `jev` from `host_rule`. Keep cold starts, failures, environmental blocks, and manual recovery in reports. A deterministic replay baseline is not the full autonomous Codex baseline. Passing a familiar task with prepared selectors is not open-ended exploration.
+
+Run `node --test scripts/tests/test_official_browser.mjs` in addition to the Python tests. Browser task selectors require refresh when website markup or locale changes.
+
+## Verified stages and diagnostics (2026-09-20)
+
+Actions may declare `dependsOn: ["earlier-action-id"]` and `once: true`. A dependency is satisfied only after that action executes and its explicit `after` assertion is observed. Every referenced dependency and every `once` action must have `after`; cycles and unknown dependencies are rejected before observation. This supports host-prepared multistage flows without offering controls from later stages too early. It does not turn an unprepared task into a deterministic workflow. The existing `deterministic` plus `requires` plus sole-eligible-candidate checks still apply.
+
+Results now include `steps` (successful executor calls), `completed` (actions with observed postconditions), and `metrics`: observation count/time, decision count/time, execution time, host-rule count and selected page-context bytes. Context bytes exclude candidate payloads, goal, history and HTTP overhead; they are not billable token counts. Timings exclude host planning and cannot by themselves establish superiority over a complete agent. Failed observations and freshness checks return structured outcomes. `maxMs` prevents additional work after the deadline; an in-flight official UI call cannot be cancelled by this adapter and can exceed it.
+
+The loop compares page states with volatile AX numeric indices removed. Reindexing alone cannot authorize replay of an ineffective action. An empty eligible action set returns `no_candidates` without calling Jev. At most three consecutive WAIT observations are accepted. Rate-limit and verification heading checks run again before action and while checking transitions; these remain narrow recognizers, not comprehensive page classification. Multiple rendered web areas require a more narrowly scoped target. English `web area`/`WebArea` and address-bar labels are additionally recognized.
+
+The candidate resolver parses nodes once per iteration and caches selector regexes per task. Candidate descriptions intentionally remain both in the observed context and in offered actions: a six-fixture live API check showed confidence regressions when removed from context. Do not report that experimental compression as an improvement. The test was small and synthetic, not a browser success-rate estimate.
